\details{
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Environmental space.} The predictor values of the backgrounds of
all groups are pooled and summarised by a principal component
analysis (the PCA of \code{\link[=thin_environment]{thin_environment()}}, precipitation amounts
log-transformed); the first two axes form the space (\code{space = "pca"}). With \code{space = "raw"}, two standardised predictors are used
instead (\code{vars}).
\item \strong{Densities.} The space is cut into a \code{grid} x \code{grid} lattice; the
densities of records and of available environments (the background)
of each group are estimated with a Gaussian kernel (reference
bandwidth, as in ecospat). The occupancy is the density of records
divided by the density of the environment (\code{correct = TRUE},
Broennimann et al. 2012), so that common environments do not look
preferred.
\item \strong{Overlap.} Schoener's \verb{D = 1 - 0.5 * sum(|p1 - p2|)} and Warren's
\code{I = 1 - 0.5 * sum((sqrt(p1) - sqrt(p2))^2)}, from the two
occupancies scaled to sum to 1: 0 = no overlap, 1 = identical. This is
the corrected Hellinger index \code{I_cor} of Rodder and Engler (2011),
used by ecospat and ENMTools; the original form \code{1 - H / 2} (Warren et
al. 2008) cannot fall below 0.29.
\item \strong{Breadth.} Levins' B2 (standardised inverse Simpson index of the
occupancy over the cells with available environment): 0 = one cell,
1 = all cells equally.
\item \strong{Niche dynamics} of the second group relative to the first (Guisan
et al. 2014): \code{expansion} (share of the second group's occupancy in
environments the first does not occupy), \code{stability} (the rest) and
\code{unfilling} (share of the first group's occupancy in environments the
second does not occupy), within the environments available to both.
\code{marginal_env} removes rare environments (e.g. 0.25 for the 75th
percentile used by Lo Parrino et al. 2023), \code{marginal_records} the
most marginal records (e.g. 0.05 for 95\% of the records).
}

\strong{Tests} (\code{n_rep} replicates each), reported with a null distribution,
p-values and a standardised effect size (\verb{(observed - null mean) / null SD}), as in \code{\link[=null_model_test]{null_model_test()}} and \code{\link[=transfer_bias_test]{transfer_bias_test()}}:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Equivalency} (identity) test: the records of both groups are pooled
and randomly re-split into groups of the same sizes (Warren et al.
2008). \code{p_lower} small = the niches are less alike than random splits:
not equivalent.
\item \strong{Similarity} (background) test, in both directions: the occupancy of
one group is compared with that of random points from the background
of the other (Warren et al. 2008, ENMTools). \code{p_higher} small = more
similar than expected from the environments available (niche
conservatism); \code{p_lower} small = less similar (divergence).
